 the truth um so to  reiterate this point in heisenberg and  certainly in principle  it is not in contradiction with the fact  that quantum mechanics  and science in general is very much  deterministic and we can get ever closer  to our truth  in heisenberg assertive principle the  uncertainty again is not about a lack of  determinism  in the very laws of physics in our very  understanding of the universe it's a  lack of in  determinism in the macroscopical  observable  so to my point of view there's no reason  to believe that we cannot get ever  closer  and more precise in our description of  nature and possibly  towards a fundamental truth hillary  i i imagine you have a slightly  different view over to you yeah  thank you james so um  well you referred to heisenberg of  course he was amongst the most  influential scientists of the 20th  century  and his account of quantum mechanics the  copenhagen interpretation  is the model that he generally used to  understand particle physics and  therefore  the building blocks of matter but he was  also a philosopher  he wrote a book uh physics and  philosophy which laid out his views not  only about the philosophy of  quantum mechanics but the philosophy of  uh  more broadly including the history of  philosophy and in it he argues that  quantum mechanics has shown that exact  science  is possible without what uh he describes  as the assumption of metaphysical  realism  by which he means that the theories of  science do describe  some existent things out there  and he goes on to say that in quantum  mechanics and i quote  objective reality has evaporated  now strangely you might think um  his philosophical work has not been  widely covered by  uh philosophers and but on a little bit  of reflection  maybe it's not so strange when he was  initially writing  um philosophy certainly in  britain and north america was largely  the mainstream  would have held a sort of scientific  view that is on two fronts on the one  hand thinkin